# 802. Find Eventual Safe States

- **LeetCode:** https://leetcode.com/problems/find-eventual-safe-states/
- **Topic:** Graph, DFS, Cycle Detection, Three-State DFS
- **Language:** Java
- **Status:** Solved

## 1. My Intuition

We need to find the nodes that are not part of a cycle.

Initially, I thought of maintaining an array containing 0 and 1. If a cycle is detected, we mark the corresponding nodes as 1. Finally, we collect the nodes that are still 0.

However, an important observation is that nodes leading to a cycle are also unsafe, even if they are not directly part of the cycle.

## 2. My Approach

Use DFS with three states:

- 0: Unvisited
- 1: Visiting or unsafe
- 2: Safe

For every node:

1. If it is already in state 1, a cycle is detected, so return false.
2. If it is in state 2, it is already confirmed safe, so return true.
3. Mark the current node as 1.
4. Perform DFS on all its adjacent nodes.
5. If any adjacent node is unsafe, the current node is also unsafe.
6. If all adjacent nodes are safe, mark the current node as 2.

Finally, collect all nodes marked as 2.

## 3. Where I Got Stuck / My Mistake

Initially, I considered marking only the nodes directly involved in a cycle.

The important correction was that a node is also unsafe if it can reach a cycle.

For example, if 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 2, nodes 0 and 1 are also unsafe.

## 4. What Clicked

The three-state DFS technique from Course Schedule (207) can also be used here.

The difference is that we need to identify safe nodes instead of simply checking whether a cycle exists.

## 5. Final Submitted Code

Reference implementation:

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph) {
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] vis = new int[n];

        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (dfs(graph, i, vis)) {
                ans.add(i);
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }

    boolean dfs(int[][] graph, int node, int[] vis) {
        if (vis[node] == 1) return false;
        if (vis[node] == 2) return true;

        vis[node] = 1;

        for (int curr : graph[node]) {
            if (!dfs(graph, curr, vis)) {
                return false;
            }
        }

        vis[node] = 2;
        return true;
    }
}

## 6. Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(V + E)
- Space Complexity: O(V), including the visited array and recursion stack.

## 7. Revision Notes

- A safe node is one from which every possible path eventually terminates.
- Nodes that can reach a cycle are also unsafe.
- Three-state DFS helps detect cycles and identify safe nodes.
- Revisit the difference between visiting and fully processed nodes.
